I was asked for the worst-case time complexity of this:

```python
def has_pair(xs, target):
    for i in range(len(xs)):
        for j in range(i + 1, len(xs)):
            if xs[i] + xs[j] == target:
                return True
    return False
```

I answered O(n) because there is only one `return True` and it usually exits early.

Grader A marked it wrong: "worst case ignores early exit by definition — with no matching pair the nested loops run n(n-1)/2 times, which is Theta(n^2). Amortised or expected-case reasoning does not apply to a worst-case question." Grader B accepted O(n), saying "in practice it returns quickly on most inputs."

